1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:06,512
The brush uncovers a bull, carved on a polished stone capital that once stood on top of a pillar.

2
00:00:10,267 --> 00:00:15,857
It is Mauryan, and the polish on it is so fine that for a long time people argued about how it was done.

3
00:00:15,857 --> 00:00:21,214
The king who put these up left something rarer than monuments, though — he left his own words.

4
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:28,594
This is the first Indian ruler we can quote directly.

5
00:00:28,594 --> 00:00:33,694
His edicts are carved on rocks and pillars across the subcontinent, in the first person.

6
00:00:33,694 --> 00:00:38,221
Buddhist tradition adds a great deal, and later travellers describe the sites.

7
00:00:38,221 --> 00:00:42,819
When the edicts and the tradition disagree, historians go with the edicts.

8
00:00:48,033 --> 00:00:52,656
Ashoka came to the throne about 268 BCE, the grandson of Chandragupta.

9
00:00:52,656 --> 00:00:57,254
Buddhist tradition says the succession was contested and violent.

10
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:06,063
The edicts say nothing about that, which is itself worth noticing: he tells us what he wants us to know.

11
00:01:10,033 --> 00:01:15,269
Then Kalinga, about 261 BCE. His own thirteenth rock edict gives the figures:

12
00:01:15,269 --> 00:01:22,954
a hundred and fifty thousand deported, a hundred thousand killed, and many times that number dead afterwards.

13
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:31,553
And then it says something no other ancient conqueror says in his own inscription — that he felt remorse, deep sorrow and regret.

14
00:01:31,553 --> 00:01:36,667
A king carved his own change of heart into rock and left it where everyone could read it.

15
00:01:41,533 --> 00:01:44,232
After that he promotes what he calls dhamma.

16
00:01:44,232 --> 00:01:49,870
Read the edicts and you find what he means by it: obedience to parents, respect for teachers,

17
00:01:49,870 --> 00:01:58,267
generosity to Brahmins and ascetics alike, kindness to servants, truthfulness, restraint in spending, and non-injury to living beings.

18
00:02:02,767 --> 00:02:09,417
Also tolerance — one edict says plainly that honouring your own sect while disparaging another's harms your own.

19
00:02:09,417 --> 00:02:13,130
It is strikingly practical, and strikingly non-sectarian.

20
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:21,653
And he built an administration for it.

21
00:02:21,653 --> 00:02:28,701
He appointed dhamma-mahamatras, officers whose job was to promote it and look after the welfare of different communities.

22
00:02:28,701 --> 00:02:33,969
He replaced royal hunting tours with dhamma-yatras, tours of inspection and teaching.

23
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:46,164
He records planting trees along roads, digging wells, building rest houses, and arranging medical treatment for people and animals.

24
00:02:46,164 --> 00:02:51,640
He also sent missions abroad, including to Sri Lanka and to Hellenistic kings he names.

25
00:02:56,967 --> 00:02:58,395
What was dhamma, then?

26
00:02:58,395 --> 00:03:05,647
One reading takes it as his Buddhism, made public: he was a lay Buddhist, he says so, and the content overlaps.

27
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:15,015
The other reading notes that the edicts addressed to the general public never mention the Four Noble Truths,

28
00:03:15,015 --> 00:03:16,669
the Eightfold Path or nirvana,

29
00:03:16,669 --> 00:03:22,431
and argues dhamma was a deliberately non-sectarian civic ethic for governing an empire of many communities.

30
00:03:22,431 --> 00:03:26,572
The evidence genuinely supports both emphases, and good answers say so.

31
00:03:33,267 --> 00:03:35,987
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

32
00:03:35,987 --> 00:03:37,314
One war, then none.

33
00:03:37,314 --> 00:03:39,937
Kalinga is the hinge of the reign.

34
00:03:45,067 --> 00:03:45,882
Dig Quiz.

35
00:03:45,882 --> 00:03:47,432
Pause and name it.

36
00:03:47,432 --> 00:03:54,039
First: the war of about 261 BCE that he describes with remorse in his own edict.

37
00:03:54,039 --> 00:03:55,074
Kalinga.

38
00:03:58,433 --> 00:04:03,361
Second: the officers he appointed to promote dhamma and look after communities.

39
00:04:03,361 --> 00:04:05,077
The dhamma-mahamatras.

40
00:04:09,333 --> 00:04:14,320
Third: the tours of inspection and teaching that replaced royal hunting trips.

41
00:04:14,320 --> 00:04:15,267
Dhamma-yatras.

42
00:04:15,267 --> 00:04:19,417
In Mains, a frequent question asks what Ashoka's dhamma was.

43
00:04:23,133 --> 00:04:29,502
Quote the edicts for its content, give both scholarly readings, and note what the public edicts leave out.

44
00:04:32,667 --> 00:04:37,009
A bull from Rampurva takes its place in the gallery, standing on its lotus.

45
00:04:46,533 --> 00:04:53,456
Next gallery: the edicts — where they are, what scripts they use, and how a forgotten king was read again.

46
00:04:53,456 --> 00:04:57,444
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

